id: pm-jkbqt8
title: "Ordering recorded as blocks is invisible to actionability: the same prerequisite schedules differently depending on which endpoint wrote it"
description: "The write path accepts an ordering constraint in two directions and only one of them reaches the scheduler.\n\ncollectBlockedByIds in src/core/item/actionability.ts resolves blockers from exactly two sources: the legacy scalar blocked_by field, and dependency rows whose kind equals the single literal BLOCKED_BY_DEPENDENCY_KIND declared at line 17. Its own docstring at line 31 states it is \"the single source of truth for what must close before this item can proceed, shared by pm next readiness classification and the close-time auto-unblock sweep\". The reverse spelling of the same relation, kind blocks, is an accepted member of the fifteen-spelling dependency vocabulary, is stored verbatim, and is honoured by the graph engine — yet it is invisible to every consumer of that single source of truth.\n\nProved in an isolated sandbox with two fresh items. Writing the constraint from the blocker side, B --dep id=A,kind=blocks, makes pm graph predecessors A return B and pm graph successors B return A, so the graph engine agrees that B precedes A. In the same workspace pm next reports A as ready with blocked count zero. Writing the identical constraint from the dependent side, C --dep id=D,kind=blocked_by, correctly reports C blocked by D. The asymmetry is silent in both directions: the blocked_by write leaves no mirror row on D, so reverse resolution exists for blocked_by alone.\n\nLive corpus impact measured over every tracked item file: 219 blocks rows exist. Five active items are gated by a blocks source that is still open, and in all five the recorded gate is absent from the blocker set that pm next reports. pm-p1g43q is classified fully ready today while pm-07pt16, still open, declares blocks against it. The remaining four are latent rather than harmless: pm-3oqjnc reports blockers pm-lvyd only and omits pm-2vs1za, pm-li2fj5 omits pm-8bgeye, pm-qd6gvf omits pm-8bgeye, pm-pfqi omits pm-q7qojt. Each becomes wrongly ready the moment its unrelated blocked_by blocker closes, and nothing will flag it.\n\nThe multi-agent consequence is the reason this matters beyond hygiene. Two agents on two branches can record the same real prerequisite from opposite ends. The field-aware merge preserves both rows without conflict, exactly as designed, and the merged workspace then schedules the work differently depending on which endpoint happened to author the edge. Correctness of the queue becomes a function of authoring style rather than of the recorded constraint.\n\nThis is a conformance gap, not a missing feature. The closed ADR pm-9x6k defines ready as an active leaf with no open blocked_by, so the single-direction model was chosen deliberately; the write path then grew to accept the reverse spelling without canonicalizing it, which is the lexicon problem pm-4020c5 owns. pm-uxkf closed by unifying next, context and list-blocked onto one edge-aware definition, so all three surfaces now inherit the same blind spot from one place — which is also why one fix repairs all three. The downstream unblocks list built from the same reverse map is blind for the same reason.\n\nScope is the resolution rule and its canonicalization, not new vocabulary. Either the write path canonicalizes blocks into a blocked_by row on the referenced item, the way depends_on already canonicalizes, or blocker resolution reads the reverse direction of ordering kinds from the graph registry that already models it. Whichever is chosen, the 219 existing rows need a stated disposition and a check that keeps the two directions from diverging again."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 1
tags[3]: actionability,graph,multi-agent
