comments[10]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-28T14:55:36.497Z","harness:claude-code","Reproducer, verified 2026-07-28 in an isolated sandbox with PM_PATH and PM_GLOBAL_PATH outside the repo.\n\nTwo fresh Tasks, A and B. One mutation: pm update B --dep id=A,kind=blocks.\n\nGraph engine agrees the ordering exists: pm graph predecessors A returns count 1 ids [B]; pm graph successors B returns count 1 ids [A]; pm graph successors A and predecessors B are both empty.\n\nScheduler disagrees: pm next reports recommended B, ready [A], blocked [], summary blocked 0. A is offered as work while its recorded predecessor is open.\n\nMirrored control in the same workspace: pm update C --dep id=D,kind=blocked_by yields pm next blocked [(C, blockers [D])], correct. pm get D shows dependencies [] — the blocked_by write stores no mirror row, so reverse resolution is implemented for blocked_by only and the two spellings are not interchangeable.\n\nLive census method, over every tracked item .toon file rather than a list projection: parse the dependencies[N]{id,kind,...} header then consume indented CSV rows until the first non-indented line. Declared rows total 8,012 with kinds related 4,930, implements 2,014, parent 275, discovered_from 220, blocks 219, verifies 139, blocked_by 127, supersedes 55, related_to 16, child 15, incident_from 2. Do not reconcile these against pm graph audit profile.edges_by_kind, which reports the registry graph after read-side canonicalization and after folding the parent scalar into edges; the two series are not comparable and never have been.\n\nNote the ratio the census exposes: 219 blocks rows against 127 declared blocked_by rows. The spelling the scheduler cannot see is the more common of the two."
  "2026-07-28T15:00:22.462Z","harness:claude-code","Third consequence of the asymmetry, discovered by accident while wiring this item and worth keeping because it closes off the obvious workaround.\n\nThe natural mitigation an agent would reach for is to record the constraint from both ends, so that the graph engine and the scheduler each see the spelling they understand. That does not work. Adding blocks rows on pm-4020c5 against this item and against pm-1w6scp, while the mirrored blocked_by rows already existed on the two dependents, immediately produced two regressions on a previously clean tracker: pm graph audit went from 11 findings to 12 with a new duplicate_edge finding, and pm validate gained a new warning class, validate_lifecycle_dependency_cycles with count 1. Both rows named the same direction — pm-4020c5 precedes the dependent — so the pair is not reciprocal and asserts no contradiction, yet the lifecycle checker canonicalizes the reverse spelling and then sees an edge in each direction between the same two nodes.\n\nThe rows were reverted and both signals returned to baseline, 11 findings and six warning classes, confirming the pair was the sole cause.\n\nSo the three spellings of one prerequisite each fail differently: blocked_by alone is correct but invisible to the graph consumers that read the other direction, blocks alone is invisible to the scheduler, and both together are a validation error. There is no way for an author to express an ordering constraint that every consumer reads correctly, which is why the fix has to be canonicalization at the write path rather than guidance about which spelling to prefer."
  "2026-07-31T14:15:57.070Z","harness:claude-code","Companion finding on the write and lifecycle side, filed 2026-07-31 as pm-xm0id4. Together the two items describe a trap with no correct move for an author.\n\nThis item proves the read-side asymmetry: a prerequisite recorded as blocks is honoured by the graph engine and invisible to collectBlockedByIds, so it never reaches readiness classification. The new finding proves the lifecycle-side asymmetry on the other spelling: clearClosedBlockerSignals at src/cli/commands/close.ts:626 deletes every blocked_by row when the item holding it closes, so the spelling that IS visible to the scheduler does not survive into history.\n\nThe corpus shows both halves at once. blocked_by is held by 91 of 318 open items and by exactly 1 of 1,900 closed items. blocks is held by 4 open items and 189 closed ones. So the two spellings partition the corpus almost perfectly by lifecycle state, and neither one is both actionable while open and durable after close. An author who picks blocked_by gets scheduling and no history; an author who picks blocks gets history and no scheduling.\n\nThat sharpens this item's multi-agent argument. Two agents recording the same real prerequisite from opposite ends do not merely schedule differently, as this item establishes - they also leave records with different lifetimes. After both items close, the branch that wrote blocks still has the edge and the branch that wrote blocked_by does not, so the field-aware merge preserves an asymmetry that was never authored as one.\n\nWhichever disposition this item chooses for the 219 blocks rows should be decided jointly with pm-xm0id4's retention semantics, because canonicalizing blocks into blocked_by rows - one of the two options this item names - would move 189 surviving historical edges into the field that the close path clears, and the next close of any of those items would delete them."
  "2026-08-08T08:12:36.051Z","harness:claude-code","The declared 241 ceiling fired against a maintenance agent on 2026-08-08 and blocked a release dry run at 243. Both new rows were mine, added minutes earlier while enriching this session's lineage, and both were wrong in exactly the way this item describes: the intended relation was that a general defect was discovered from two specific ones, which the graph already carried as discovered_from, and blocks was reached for because it is the reflex spelling for any ordering intuition. Neither edge expressed a prerequisite and neither would have scheduled anything. This is useful evidence for the item's thesis rather than for its count. The population does not grow because anyone decides ordering should be recorded as blocks; it grows because blocks is the default reach when a relation feels directional, and nothing at write time asks whether a prerequisite is actually being asserted or from which endpoint. Rows removed by exact id and kind selector, restoring 241; the ratchet then passed at 26 enforced bounds."
  "2026-08-08T22:53:20.661Z","harness:codex","Implementation start: blocks mutations are above the filed ceiling and reverse ordering remains invisible to readiness. This tranche will make actionability consume the canonical relationship registry so blocks and blocked_by have identical scheduling semantics."
  "2026-08-08T23:01:29.313Z","harness:codex","Implementation evidence: reverse blocks edges now feed the same blocker resolver, blocked classification, downstream unblocks projection, and close-time auto-unblock candidate path as blocked_by. Mixed-case reverse targets and bidirectional readiness are covered directly."
  "2026-08-08T23:24:57.781Z","harness:codex","Live acceptance evidence: pm list-blocked --no-truncate now includes pm-p1g43q while pm-07pt16 remains open and declares blocks -> pm-p1g43q. The conformance test enumerates every built-in registry ordering kind and requires exact coverage by ACTIONABILITY_ORDERING_KINDS, so adding an accepted ordering spelling without blocker resolution fails CI."
  "2026-08-09T00:09:11.138Z","harness:codex","Final verification: the complete sandboxed coverage gate passed 461/461 files and 7086/7086 tests at exact 100/100/100/100, including reverse blocks readiness, unblocks, close actionability, blank legacy identifiers, and registry conformance."
  "2026-08-09T00:20:20.195Z","harness:codex","Delivery handoff: broad SDK/CLI tranche committed at 5b36abc75 and opened as GitHub PR #944 (https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/pull/944). Exact local evidence includes 7,086 tests across 461 files at 100/100/100/100 coverage, package/compatibility/npx/dogfood/manual acceptance, tracker/changelog/SDK/static-quality gates, and zero current dependency or GitHub security alerts. Review and exact-head CI evidence will be appended after the hosted loop."
  "2026-08-09T00:58:12.916Z","harness:codex","Review-loop evidence: normalized and deduplicated reverse blocks identifiers through a one-pass shared corpus index used by actionability and close auto-unblock, removing the prior repeated corpus scan. Missing reverse targets and mixed-case IDs are covered. Full sandboxed gate: 461 files, 7,089 tests, exact 100/100/100/100."
